+    // ---- materialising a CR0 test as a 0/1 value ----
+    //
+    // mfocrf moves one CR field into a GPR; the field lands in the same bit
+    // positions it occupies in the 32-bit CR, so CR0's LT/GT/EQ/SO are at
+    // big-endian bit positions 0/1/2/3, i.e. little-endian 31/30/29/28. A
+    // rotate of (bit + 1) brings the wanted one to bit 0 and the 31,31 mask
+    // discards the rest. This is the same sequence offlineasm/ppc64.rb uses
+    // for `setcr`.
+    // `dest` is the staging register, not dataTempRegister. It can be, because
+    // the CR field is not a GPR: by the time this runs the comparison that set
+    // it has already read its operands for the last time, so nothing this
+    // writes can still be wanted -- including when `dest` aliases one of them.
+    //
+    // It HAS to be, because Air generates under
+    // DisallowMacroScratchRegisterUsage (AirGenerate.cpp:183,
+    // AirAllocateRegistersAndStackAndGenerateCode.cpp:540), the allocator may
+    // have handed r29 or r30 to a live Tmp, and this is the tail of every
+    // compare32, compare64, test32, test64, compareDouble and compareFloat --
+    // which is to say, of the two most common opcodes this target claims a
+    // form for. Reaching for the temp here cost 12 of a 100-case testb3
+    // failure sample.
+    void emitSetFromCR0(CRTest test, RegisterID dest)
+    {
+        unsigned fieldIndex = static_cast<unsigned>(test.field);
+        // FXM is a one-hot field selector in big-endian bit order, so CR0 is
+        // 0x80 and CR7 is 0x01.
+        m_assembler.mfocrf(dest, 0x80u >> fieldIndex);
+        // mfocrf leaves the field in the bit positions it occupies in the
+        // 32-bit CR, so the wanted bit is at big-endian position
+        // fieldIndex * 4 + bit; rotating left by one more than that brings
+        // it to bit 0.
+        m_assembler.rlwinm(dest, dest, crBitIndex(test.field, test.bit) + 1, 31, 31);
+        if (!test.branchIfSet)
+            m_assembler.xori(dest, dest, 1);
+    }
